 "title": "What caused each connection",
 "review": "unchecked",
 "review_note": "Every row was written by hand from the standard accounts, with the sources named. A second reader went back over the rows in three passes. On 18 November 2026 the first pass started with the 33 rows marked “less sure” and then took the rows that connect the most traditions: 34 rows were read against pages that were opened (the Library’s own texts and English Wikipedia pages, some of them read through a summary), 42 rows were changed in all, and no row was removed. On 21 November 2026 a second reader, another working session, read 54 more rows: 19 against the Library’s own texts, 5 of the “less sure” rows against the page they cite, and 30 against the opening section of the English Wikipedia page the row cites (the openings give the main dates and names, not every detail); 23 rows were changed, 2 of them on other grounds (a search summary), and no row was removed. On 22 November 2026 a third reader, again another working session, read the last 91 rows, each against the whole text of the English Wikipedia pages it cites and of other pages found for it, and 16 of them also against the Library’s own texts; 56 rows were changed, 10 more had a source added, the rest were found as written, and no row was removed. In all 179 rows were read against pages that were opened. Where a page did not say what a row said, the row was narrowed and says so. All the Wikipedia addresses were looked up in the first pass: ten were replaced because the page does not exist, is a list of meanings or leads somewhere else. Every one of the 179 rows has now been read a second time by another reader; a row marked “less sure” carries a note saying what is still to be looked at. A second reader is not a specialist: no specialist has read these rows, and nothing here is a final reading.",
 "not_recorded": "How this came about is not recorded here.",
 "link_note": "A row is about one recorded connection (its id), or, written “lineage:” and a tradition’s id, about the link the map data records between that tradition and the one it grew from where no connection says so. A link or connection with no row has no cause recorded.",
 "rule": "A cause is what brought two traditions into contact, or made one grow out of another, as the sources report it, with how well each is documented. It is not a verdict, and it does not say who was right. A connection with no cause here has none recorded, which is not the same as having none.",
